      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the chicken thighs in a large saucepan with the onion, garlic, ground coriander, cumin, cinnamon and black pepper. Cover with 500ml water and bring to the boil. Reduce to a simmer, cover and cook for 35-40 minutes until the chicken is cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the chicken and allow to cool enough to handle. Pull the meat from the bones and shred it into small pieces. Discard the skin and bones."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the cooking liquid to the hob and bring to a boil over high heat. Reduce for 5 minutes until it thickens slightly. Reduce the heat to low and pour in the beaten eggs, stirring constantly until they form soft curds. Remove from the heat, stir in the parsley and fresh coriander, then fold in the shredded chicken."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Preheat the oven to 190C. In a small bowl, mix the toasted almonds with 1 tbsp icing sugar and 0.5 tsp ground cinnamon."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Brush a 30cm round or rectangular baking tin with melted butter. Layer 4-5 sheets of filo in the tin, brushing each with butter and letting the edges overhang. Spread the almond mixture over the base. Spoon the chicken filling on top and spread evenly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fold the overhanging pastry up over the filling. Layer 3-4 more buttered filo sheets over the top, tucking the edges under the pie to seal. Brush the top generously with the remaining butter."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bake for 25-30 minutes until deep golden and crisp. Leave to rest for 5 minutes, then dust thickly with icing sugar and a light sprinkle of cinnamon. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The pastry is soggy on the bottom"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The filling must be as dry as possible before assembly. If the chicken mixture looks wet, tip it into a colander lined with a clean cloth and squeeze out any excess liquid before adding it to the pastry."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The top is browning too fast"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lay a loose sheet of foil over the top after 15 minutes and continue baking. Remove the foil for the last 5 minutes to allow the top to crisp up."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The egg layer is rubbery"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The eggs should be cooked over very low heat and stirred continuously. Remove the pan from the heat the moment the eggs form soft, barely-set curds. They will continue to cook from the residual heat of the filling."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chicken "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "bastilla-term"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "bastilla"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " originated in Fez and is considered one of the great dishes of Moroccan imperial cooking. It was historically made with pigeon and was a dish of the wealthy, served at the beginning of elaborate banquets. The dusting of icing sugar over savoury meat is the defining feature that surprises first-time eaters and converts them entirely. Seafood versions have become popular in coastal cities, but the chicken version remains the most widely made."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
